 "drivingQuestion": "Does 'majority wormy' refer to each kernel or to the se'a?",
 "outcome": "teiku",
 "outcomeNote": "R' Yirmeya's dilemma unresolved",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "menachot.85b.7",
   "daf": "85b",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "וְאִם הִתְלִיעָה – פְּסוּלָה. תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן: סוֹלֶת שֶׁהִתְלִיעָה רוּבָּהּ – פְּסוּלָה, וְחִיטִּין שֶׁהִתְלִיעוּ רוּבָּן – פְּסוּלוֹת. בָּעֵי רַבִּי יִרְמְיָה: בְּרוֹב חִטָּה, אוֹ בְּרוֹב סְאָה? תֵּיקוּ.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «And if» the flour «became wormy,» it is «unfit» for use in a meal offering. «The Sages taught» in a baraita: «Fine flour the majority of which became wormy» is «unfit. And» similarly, «wheat» kernels «the majority of which became wormy» are «unfit,» and they may not be used to produce fine flour for meal offerings. «Rabbi Yirmeya asks:» What is the meaning of this latter ruling? Is it saying only that if the «majority of» an individual «wheat» kernel becomes wormy the flour produced from it is unfit, «or» is it saying that when the «majority of a se’a» of kernels becomes wormy the entire se’a is «unfit?» The Gemara concludes: The dilemma «shall stand» unresolved."
  }
 ],
